<fig:examples>

As we have argued, PTA data prefers blue tilted spectra, which may be associated to the low frequency tail of the SIGW background. So, let us derive the conditions such that the amplitude at the tail is the same regardless of $c_s$. This gives a relation between $k_(upright(p))$ and $A_(cal(R))$ in terms of $c_s$. We start by deriving the asymptotic form of the SIGW spectral density @eq:spectraldensitytodaydirac for $k lt.double k_(upright(p))$, which gives

$ Omega_(upright("GW"), upright(c)) ( k lt.double k_(upright(p)) ) approx 1 / 12 (A_(cal(R)) / c_s^2 k / k_(upright(p)))^2 (pi^2 + 4 (1 + ln [frac(k, 2 c_s k_(upright(p)))])^2) thin . $<eq:IRshape>

As is clear from (#ref(<eq:IRshape>)) there is a degeneracy in the IR tail where, if we neglect the logarithmic correction,

$ A_(cal(R)) / c_s^2 k / k_(upright(p)) = upright("constant") thin . $

Given the upper bound on the amplitude of perturbations induced by PBH overproduction (that we will derive in details in the following), one obtains also a constrain on the maximum scale $k_(upright(p))$ that could be compatible with PTA observations.

We will find that the data seem to prefer that the amplitude of the smooth bump in the SIGW spectrum, i.e. the maximum that is not the resonant peak (see @fig:examples), is similar for all $c_s$. The position of the "low frequency" maximum is located at

$ k_(upright("bump")) = c_s k_(upright(p)) / sqrt(2) . $<eq:kbump>

For reference, the destructive interference minimum where $Omega_(upright("GW"), upright(c)) = 0$ is located at $k_(upright("des")) = sqrt(2) c_s k_(upright(p))$. The amplitude of the SIGW spectrum at the "low frequency" maximum is given by

$ Omega_(upright("GW"), upright(c))^(upright("bump")) = frac(3, 32 c_s^2) (1 - c_s^2 / 8)^2 (frac(9 pi^2, 64) + (1 - frac(3 ln 7, 8))^2) approx 0.14 A_(cal(R))^2 times frac(( 1 - 0.12 c_s^2 )^2, c_s^2) thin . $<eq:bump>

From @eq:bump, we obtain that for a given $c_s$ the amplitude of the SIGW spectrum at the bump is related to the case of $c_s = sqrt(w) = 1 / sqrt(3)$ via

$ frac(Omega_(upright("GW"), upright(0)) h^2 ( c_s ), Omega_(upright("GW"), upright(0)) h^2 ( c_s = sqrt(w) )) prop A_(cal(R) , c_s)^2 / A_(cal(R) , sqrt(w))^2 w / c_s^2 thin , $

where the subscript $c_s$ and $sqrt(w)$ respectively refers to a given parameter. For example, $A_(cal(R) , sqrt(w))$ refers to $A_(cal(R))$ in the case when $c_s = sqrt(w)$. We also need to require that the position of the bump @eq:kbump is the same for different $c_s$. With this information, we find that the requirement that the low frequency tail and the peak are similar for all models is given by

$ A_(cal(R) , c_s) approx c_s / sqrt(w) times A_(cal(R) , sqrt(w)) quad upright("and") quad k_(upright(p) , c_s) approx c_s / sqrt(w) times k_(upright(p) , sqrt(w)) thin . $<eq:relations>

We show the low frequency tail of all spectra coincides on the right plot of @fig:examples. This roughly agrees with the degeneracy we later find in the posteriors of the Bayesian inference on the NANOGrav and EPTA data, namely a degeneracy along $c_s^(- 1) times A_(cal(R)) = upright("constant")$ and $k_(upright(p)) times c_s = upright("constant")$. We now proceed to investigate the effect of $c_s$ on PBH formation.

= PRIMORDIAL BLACK HOLES
<primordial-black-holes>
As we are considering sharp log-normal peaks for the SIGWs, we focus to a good approximation on the case of Dirac delta spectrum @eq:pdirac for the PBH counterpart. For definiteness, we will use log-normal @eq:PRLN with $Delta = 0.1$ in our numerical calculations.

In order to gain an intuition on the order of magnitude, the typical PBH mass can be estimated to be @Sasaki:2018dmp

$ M_(upright("PBH")) = 4 pi epsilon.alt M_(upright("pl"))^2 / H_(upright(p)) thin , $

where $M_(upright("pl"))$ is the reduced Planck mass, $H_(upright(p))$ is the Hubble parameter when the mode $k_(upright(p))$ enters the Hubble radius ($k_(upright(p)) = a_(upright(p)) H_(upright(p))$) and $epsilon.alt$ is the fraction of the Hubble volume that goes into the PBH and has to be determined numerically. Assuming standard cosmological evolution @Planck:2018vyg, one has

$ M_(upright("PBH"), upright(f)) approx 6.7 times 10^(- 4) M_⊙ (frac(k_(upright(p)), 10^8 thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)))^(- 2) (epsilon.alt / 0.3) thin , $

where $M_⊙ approx 2 times 10^33 thin upright(g)$ is a solar mass. Notice that the precise value of $epsilon.alt$ is derived adopting the critical collapse formula, informed wih the results of numerical simulations (see e.g. @Musco:2008hv and more details in App.~@pbh-abundance-using-peaks-and-press-schecter). The fraction of PBHs as dark matter can be estiamted to be

$ f_(upright("PBH")) approx 1.4 times 10^10 thin beta thin (frac(k_(upright(p)), 10^8 thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1))) (frac(g_rho ( T_(upright(p)) ), 10.75))^(3 / 4) (frac(g_s ( T_(upright(p)) ), 10.75))^(- 1) thin , $

where $beta$ is the energy density fraction of PBHs at formation and $T_(upright(p))$ is the temperature at $H_(upright(p))$. Note that in the case of scalar field domination we should replace $T_(upright(p))$ by $T_(upright(c))$ but it is a minor effect which we neglect.

We compute the PBH abundance in detail taking into account the critical collapse and non-linearities as in Refs.~@Young:2019yug @DeLuca:2019qsy @Gow:2020bzo @Young:2022phe @Ferrante:2022mui. We perform the computation using both Peak Theory and Press-Schecter, in order to account for the uncertainties related to the choice of method, which results on slightly different prediction for the PBH abundance @Young:2014ana. We provide all the formulas and parameters used in App.~@pbh-abundance-using-peaks-and-press-schecter and show the numerical results in @fig:fpbh.

To understand the effect of a change in $c_s$, it is instructive to use a simplified Press-Schecter approach without the inclusion of non-linearities and critical collapse, where we have that @Sasaki:2018dmp

$ beta approx frac(sigma_(upright("PBH")), sqrt(2 pi) delta_(upright("th"))) exp [- frac(delta_(upright("th"))^2, 2 sigma_(upright("PBH"))^2)] thin , $

where $delta_(upright("th"))$ is the threshold for critical collapse, which we take to be $delta_(upright("th")) = 0.59$ @Musco:2020jjb, and

$ sigma_(upright("PBH"))^2 = 16 / 81 integral_0^oo frac(upright(d) k, k) ( k r_m )^4 W^2 ( k r_m ) cal(P)_(cal(R)) ( k ) , $

with $W^2 ( k r_m )$ is the window function. We use the real space top-hat window function, which is consistent with the smoothing adopted to determine the threshold for collapse in @Musco:2020jjb, and given in App.~@pbh-abundance-using-peaks-and-press-schecter.

At this simple level, the only effect of $c_s$ is to increase or decrease $delta_(upright("th"))$. The naive expectation is that increasing $c_s$ would raise the threshold. The actual effect of $c_s$ on $delta_(upright("th"))$ for a fixed $w$ has not been studied with numerical simulations, but for $c_s^2 = 1$ we follow Carr's estimate @Carr:1975qj, that on constant Hubble slices leads to @Domenech:2020ers

$ delta_(upright("th")) = frac(3 ( 1 + w ), 5 + 3 w) c_s^2 = frac(3 ( 1 + w ), 5 + 3 w) = 2 / 3 thin . $

This corresponds to the maximum value for the threshold one can get by saturating the compaction function. However, choosing $delta_(upright("th")) = 2 / 3$ exactly leaves no room for type-I PBH formation in the critical collapse formulation @Musco:2020jjb. We therefore take $delta_(upright("th")) ( c_s = 1 ) approx 0.66$.#footnote[We also checked that slightly lowering the threshold to $0.65$ does not affect our results in any relevant way, which shows the PBH suppression is not due to the threshold being close to the edge of type-I parameter space.] As this only represents an estimate for $c_s = 1$, further numerical investigations are required to determine the precise value of the threshold in these scenarios. We note that the case of generic $c_s^2 = w$ has been studied in Ref.~@Musco:2012au @Escriva:2020tak but it is not possible to extrapolate to general $c_s$ from their analysis.

