<fig:fpbh>

As the shape of smoothed power spectrum remains the same when varying the equation of state, while only its amplitude varies, we can compare the PBH abundance in the general case of $c_s$ with the well studied case of $c_s = sqrt(w)$. By doing so, we obtain

$ frac(f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s ), f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s = sqrt(w) )) approx delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w)) / delta_(upright("th") , c_s) k_(upright(p) , c_s) / k_(upright(p) , sqrt(w)) sqrt(A_(cal(R) , c_s) / A_(cal(R) , sqrt(w))) e^(- frac(delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w))^2, 2 sigma_(upright("PBH") , sqrt(w))^2) (A_(cal(R) , sqrt(w)) / A_(cal(R) , c_s) delta_(upright("th") , c_s)^2 / delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w))^2 - 1)) thin . $

Using the relations we found in the previous section, namely @eq:relations, if we require that the SIGW spectrum computed with different values of $c_s$ provides the explanation for the NANOGrav signal, i.e. the IR tail has its amplitude fixed by requiring consistency with observations irrespectively of $c_s$, the abundance of the PBH counterpart is modified as

$ frac(f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s ), f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s = sqrt(w) )) approx sqrt(c_s / sqrt(w)) delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w)) / delta_(upright("th") , c_s) e^(- frac(delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w))^2, 2 sigma_(upright("PBH") , sqrt(w))^2) (sqrt(w) / c_s delta_(upright("th") , c_s)^2 / delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w))^2 - 1)) thin . $<eq:comparison>

We therefore conclude that the abundance is suppressed with respect to $c_s = sqrt(w)$, that is $f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s ) < f_(upright("PBH"))^(upright("PS")) ( c_s = sqrt(w) )$, when

$ delta_(upright("th") , c_s) > (c_s / sqrt(w))^(1 / 2) times delta_(upright("th") , sqrt(w)) thin , $<eq:inequalitydelta>

because of the exponential dependence in @eq:comparison.

The inequality @eq:inequalitydelta saturates for $delta_(upright("th") , c_s) = sqrt(c_s)$. If we use Carr's estimate, namely $delta_(upright("th") , c_s) prop c_s^2$, we conclude that PBH abundance is always suppressed for larger propagation speed. But Carr‘s value may not a very good estimate in general. For practical purposes, let us assume that there is a general power-law dependence on $c_s$, i.e. $delta_(upright("th") , c_s) prop c_s^n$. In that case, we find that the inequality @eq:inequalitydelta yields

$ (c_s / sqrt(w))^(2 n - 1) > 1 thin . $

For $n > 1 / 2$, the PBH abundance is suppressed if $c_s > sqrt(w)$. This is the natural expectation: although the amplitude of the primordial spectrum is also larger for larger $c_s$, if we require the same IR tail amplitude of the SIGWs @eq:relations, PBH formation is exponentially sensitive to any change. For $n = 1 / 2$ there is no change in the abundance of PBHs. The change in amplitude is exactly compensated by the change in threshold. For $n < 1 / 2$ then PBH abundance is instead suppressed if $c_s < sqrt(w)$.

In our numerical estimates, we find that $n > 1 / 2$ and PBH abundance is more suppressed with larger $c_s$. In @fig:fpbh we show the values of $A_(cal(R))$ such that $f_(upright("PBH")) = 1$ for $c_s^2 = 1 / 3$ and $c_s^2 = 1$. It can be seen that the change in the required $A_(cal(R))$ is almost one order of magnitude: from $A_(cal(R)) tilde 10^(- 2)$ for $c_s^2 = 1 / 3$ to $A_(cal(R)) tilde 10^(- 1)$ for $c_s^2 = 1$. Thus, the change in $f_(upright("PBH"))$ for $c_s^2 = 1$ largely accommodates the slightly larger amplitude needed yield the same amplitude of the GW spectrum at low frequencies, which is a factor $sqrt(3)$.

= RESULTS
<results>

We perform a Bayesian inference on the NANOGrav~@NG15-SGWB and EPTA~@EPTA2-pulsars datasets, using their posteriors distribution for $Omega_(upright("GW"))$ in the lowest 14 and 9 frequency bins, respectively, following the choices made by the collaborations. We adopt our templates @eq:OmegaDelta and @eq:spectraldensitytodaydirac, which are characterised by the free parameters in the model: $A_(cal(R))$, $k_(upright(p))$, $c_s$ and $Delta$ (the corresponding priors adopted in the analysis can be found in @tab:priors in App.~@additional-results-with-fixed-c_s2). The posteriors distributions are shown in @fig:fitPTA.

