We display our results in @fig:crossterms. The blue curve in all the panels of @fig:crossterms corresponds to the log-normal component, the purple corresponds the the cross terms between the step and log-normal component, the cyan corresponds to the step contribution and finally the red curve corresponds to the total. Importantly we see the appearance of the $f^(- 2)$ slope for the cross term distinctive of the $c_s^2 = 1$ @Balaji:2022dbi. We show the LISA @Barausse:2020rsu, $mu$Ares @Sesana:2019vho and DECIGO @Kawamura:2020pcg power-law integrated sensitivity curves along with the NANOGrav data.

Observing all the panels of @fig:crossterms we see that for the chosen $k_(upright(p))$, the log-normal peak fits the NANOGrav data well and dominates in the IR tail. However, due to the sharp characteristic cutoff at $f > 10^(- 7)$ Hz, this component will not be visible to even optimistic proposed GW experiments. However, for $A_(upright("rel")) = 10^(- 3)$ we note that cross term contribution dominates at frequencies $10^(- 7) thin upright("Hz") lt.tilde f lt.tilde 10^(- 6) thin upright("Hz")$. The cross term is also sufficiently subdominant to not interfere with the IR behaviour of the total spectrum. At frequencies above $tilde.eq 10^(- 6)$ Hz, the step term dominates and continues as a constant into the high frequency regime. We also find that the $f^(- 2)$ scaling is not enough to reach LISA which is at least $2$ decades away from the peak of the SIGWs needed to explain the PTA data. This means that the signal would be at least a factor $10^(- 4)$ suppressed at the scale corresponding to the LISA sensitivity. Since from PTAs we have that $Omega_(upright("GW"))^(upright("peak")) tilde 10^(- 7)$ at $f tilde 10^(- 7) thin upright("Hz")$, this takes us to $Omega_(upright("GW"))^(upright("cross")) < 10^(- 12)$ at $f tilde 10^(- 5) thin upright("Hz")$, right below LISA. In order to see the $f^(- 2)$ scaling we need a detector at $mu upright("Hz")$ such as $mu$Ares.

From the right and bottom panels of @fig:crossterms we see that for $A_(upright("flat")) gt.tilde 2 times 10^(- 5)$ the plateau would be visible by LISA and DECIGO. Also in that case, we will be able to see the characteristic $f^(- 2)$ signature of $c_s^2 = 1$.

For $A_(upright("flat")) lt.tilde 2 times 10^(- 5)$ the plateau would only be visible to $mu$Ares and DECIGO. While this is a compelling possibility, we would then not be able to detect the characteristic $f^(- 2)$ slope. Nevertheless, we find that for $A_(upright("flat")) > 10^(- 6)$ the plateau would be visible to $mu$Ares, probing a significantly small amplitude of primordial fluctuations, about three orders of magnitude above the CMB normalisation.

= CONCLUSIONS
<conclusions>

PTA collaborations @NG15-SGWB @NG15-pulsars @EPTA2-SGWB @EPTA2-pulsars @EPTA2-SMBHB-NP @PPTA3-SGWB @PPTA3-pulsars @PPTA3-SMBHB @CPTA-SGWB reported mounting evidence for a SGWB at nHz frequencies. If such SGWB had cosmic origins, PTAs could be actually probing the physics in a period of the unexplored early universe. Thus, we can use PTAs to test the content of the universe at the time of GW generation, in addition to the physics that lead to the GW generation.

In this paper, we considered SIGWs produced in a early universe dominated by a scalar field and focused on the impact of a different propagation speed of scalar fluctuations while fixing $w = 1 / 3$. We find that while PTA data is consistent with $c_s^2 = w = 1 / 3$ (see the posterior distributions in @fig:fitPTA), PBH overproduction constraints require $c_s^2 > 1 / 3$ (unless specific non-Gaussianities are introduced in the model~@Franciolini:2023pbf). Remarkably, if we fix $c_s^2 = 1$, the SIGWs can explain the PTA signal and predict a consistent abundance of PBHs.

Finally, we argued that a distinctive feature of $c_s^2 = 1$ in our model could be a $Omega_(upright("GW")) prop f^(- 2)$ scaling at the $mu upright("Hz")$ frequencies, which would be probed in the distant future by detectors such as $mu$Ares. We stress that such $f^(- 2)$ scaling requires additional power in the curvature fluctuations beyond the log-normal peak potentially associated to the SGWB observed with PTA. This is present in some inflationary models with sharp features (e.g. see Refs.~@Pi:2017gih @Ando:2018nge @Atal:2018neu @Chen:2019zza @Braglia:2020eai @Ragavendra:2020sop @Fumagalli:2020adf @Ng:2021hll). In these scenarios, in addition to the $f^(- 2)$ tail, we find that in general there should be a flat SIGW spectrum contribution entering the LISA and DECIGO sensitivity (see @fig:crossterms). While the SIGW plateau is also present in other models, e.g. see Ref.~@DeLuca:2020agl @Franciolini:2022pav @Ferrante:2023bgz @Firouzjahi:2023lzg, its combination with the $f^(- 2)$ scaling at $mu upright("Hz")$ may represent a peculiar characteristic of $c_s^2 = 1$. This could provide an additional way to uncover the content of the universe at the time of wave generation using GWs in PTAs, $mu$Ares, LISA and DECIGO.

