= PBH ABUNDANCE USING PEAKS AND PRESS-SCHECTER
<pbh-abundance-using-peaks-and-press-schecter>

Here we write explicitly the equations and parameters used to calculate the PBH abundance $f_(upright("PBH"))$. We follow @Young:2019yug @Gow:2020bzo. The variance of fluctuations smoothed over a scale $r_m$ is given by#footnote[Notice that uncertainties related to the choice of the window function exists, even though the impact of the choice is reduced when both the variance and the threshold are computed accordingly @Young:2019osy.]

$ sigma^2 = 16 / 81 integral_0^oo frac(upright(d) k, k) ( k r_m )^4 W^2 ( k r_m ) T^2 ( c_s k r_m ) cal(P)_(cal(R)) ( k ) , $

where $W ( k r_m )$ is the window function, which for a real space top-hat is given by

$ W ( k r_m ) = 3 frac(upright("sin") ( k r_m ) - k r_m upright("cos") ( k r_m ), ( k r_m )^3) , $

and $T ( c_s k r_m )$ is the linear transfer function, which for $w = 1 / 3$ and constant $c_s$ reads

$ T ( c_s k r_m ) = 3 frac(upright("sin") ( c_s k r_m ) - c_s k r_m upright("cos") ( c_s k r_m ), ( c_s k r_m )^3) thin . $

For a Dirac delta primordial spectrum it has been shown that $k_(upright(p)) r_m approx 2.744$ @Young:2019osy while for a log-normal @eq:PRLN with $Delta = 0.1$ we have $k_(upright(p)) r_m approx 2.7$ @Musco:2020jjb.

We will use peaks theory and threshold (Press-Schecter) statistics. For peaks, the number density of peaks is then given by

$ cal(N) ( nu ) = frac(1, 3^(3 / 2) 4 pi^2) (mu / sigma)^3 nu^3 exp (- nu^2 / 2) thin , $

where $nu = delta_l / sigma$, and

$ mu^2 = 16 / 81 integral_0^oo frac(upright(d) k, k) ( k r_m )^6 W^2 ( k r_m ) T^2 ( c_s k r_m ) cal(P)_(cal(R)) ( k ) thin . $

We include unavoidable non-linear effects by relating the linear density fluctuation $delta_l$ with the non-linear one by

$ delta_m = delta_l - 3 / 8 delta_l^2 thin . $

Taking into account critical collapse, we have that the PBH mass follows

$ M_(upright("PBH")) = cal(K) M_(upright(H)) (delta_m - delta_(upright("th")))^gamma , $

with $cal(K) = 4$ and $gamma = 0.36$ as in @Gow:2020bzo. For $c_s^2 = 1 / 3$ we take $delta_(upright("th")) = 0.59$ @Musco:2020jjb @Musco:2023dak and for $c_s^2 = 1$ we use $delta_(upright("th")) = 0.66$. This also fixes the minimum density fluctuation which can give rise to a PBH, namely

$ delta_(c , l -) = 4 / 3 (1 - sqrt(frac(2 - 3 delta_(upright("th")), 2))) thin . $

With all the above, the total fraction of PBHs at formation reads

$ beta_(upright("peaks")) = integral_(delta_(c , l -))^(4 / 3) upright(d) delta_l thin cal(K) (delta_m - delta_(upright("th")))^gamma cal(N) ( nu ) , $

where the upper integration limit comes from considering only Type-I perturbations (even though this has a very minor impact on results @Musco:2018rwt). If we use threshold (Press-Schecter) statistics, we then need to compute @Gow:2020bzo

$ beta_(upright("PS")) = integral_(delta_(c , l -))^(4 / 3) upright(d) delta_l thin cal(K) (delta_m - delta_(upright("th")))^gamma frac(1, sqrt(2 pi) sigma) exp (- nu^2 / 2) thin . $

= SCALAR FIELD MODEL WITH GENERAL CONSTANT $w$ AND $c_s$
<scalar-field-model-with-general-constant-w-and-c_s>
In this appendix we provide concrete realizations of model with constant $w$ and $c_s$. We consider a general k-essence type scalar field model @Armendariz-Picon:1999hyi @Garriga:1999vw, namely the scalar action is given by

$ S ( phi.alt ) = integral d^4 x sqrt(- g) K ( phi.alt , X ) thin , $

where $X = - 1 / 2 partial_mu phi.alt partial^mu phi.alt$. In this model we have that the energy density and pressure are given by

$ rho = 2 X K_X - K quad upright("and") quad p = K thin , $

where $K_X equiv d K / d X$. The sound speed of fluctuations reads

$ c_s^(- 2) = 1 + frac(2 X K_(X X), K_X) thin . $

The Klein-Gordon equation at the Friedmann--Lemaître--Robertson--Walker (FLRW) background leads to

$ frac(d, d t) (a^3 K_X dot(phi.alt)) = a^3 K_phi.alt thin , $<eq:KG>

where $H = dot(a) / a$ with $a$ being the scale factor and $dot(phi.alt) = d phi.alt / d t$ with $t$ the cosmic time.

The simplest model was proposed by Lucchin and Matarrese @Lucchin:1984yf and considers

$ K ( phi.alt , X ) = X - V_star.op e^(lambda phi.alt) thin . $<eq:exponential>

This choice leads to $c_s^2 = 1$ and it is easy to check that it also has a solution of the type $a prop t^p$, $H = p / t$ and $phi.alt = 2 / lambda times ln t$, where $p = 2 / lambda^2$. The equation of state is related to $p$ by

$ w = frac(2 - 3 p, 3 p) thin . $<eq:wwww>

For $p = 1 / 2$ ($lambda = 2$) we have $w = 1 / 3$.

We can also generalize this model to

$ K ( phi.alt , X ) = X^alpha - V_star.op phi.alt^beta thin . $

In this case one finds that

$ c_s^(- 2) = 2 alpha - 1 thin . $

Furthermore, requiring a solution of the type $a = a_star.op ( t / t_star.op )^p$ and $phi.alt = phi.alt_star.op ( t / t_star.op )^q$ gives a relation between $alpha$ and $beta$. They must also must satisfy the Friedmann equation, namely $3 H^2 = rho$. Equating the powers of $t$ in @eq:KG and in the Friedmann equation, we find

$ q = frac(alpha - 1, alpha) quad upright("and") quad beta = frac(2 alpha, 1 - alpha) thin . $

The equation of state for this model is again given by @eq:wwww. Solving the Klein-Gordon and Friedmann equation relates other parameters such as $V_star.op$ and $phi.alt_star.op$ with $alpha$ and $p$. In this way, we have a model with constant and independent $w$ and $c_s$. As an example, when $V_star.op = 0$ we have $c_s^2 = w$. We can also formally recover the $c_s^2 = 1$ case in the limit where $q arrow.r 0$ while keeping $phi.alt_star.op times q = upright("constant")$. Solving the Klein-Gordon and Friedmann equations yields $q phi.alt_star.op = sqrt(2 p)$, consistent with the parameters of the exponential potential (see the discussion after @eq:exponential).

= ADDITIONAL RESULTS WITH FIXED $c_s^2$
<additional-results-with-fixed-c_s2>

In this appendix we provide the results of the analysis for fixed $c_s^2 = 1 / 3$ and $c_s^2 = 1$. We take the priors shown in @tab:priors. We show the resulting posteriors in @fig:fitPTAcomparisons and the violin plots with the SIGW spectrum in @fig:spectraapp. We find that a higher $c_s$ requires a higher amplitude $A_(cal(R))$ of the primordial spectrum and a lower value of $k_(upright(p))$. As we show in @fig:pbhresults, the increase in $c_s^2$ has a bigger impact on $f_(upright("PBH"))$ than the increase of $A_(cal(R))$ leading to a fraction of PBHs consistent with the $1 sigma$ bounds from the PTA analysis. We also see from @fig:spectraapp that the data is mainly fitted by the low frequency tail of the SIGW spectrum.

